From: Adlink CCoE <dinesh.v@adlinktech.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Mar 2023 14:27:37 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Avoid ACPI probe due to resource conflict with BIOS for accessing SMBUS register

On ADLINK x86 platforms the BIOS declares the SMBus I/O ports in its ACPI
tables and continues to access them at runtime. i801_acpi_probe() installs an
ACPI address-space handler which detects this, sets priv->acpi_reserved and
inhibits all further access from the driver:

  i801_smbus 0000:00:1f.4: BIOS is accessing SMBus registers
  i801_smbus 0000:00:1f.4: Driver SMBus register access inhibited

Every subsequent transaction then returns -EBUSY, leaving the SEMA board
management controller unreachable.

Skip the ACPI probe so the driver retains ownership of the bus. Note that this
deliberately gives up the kernel's protection against concurrent BIOS and
driver access to the SMBus.

Rebased for kernel 6.18. The original 2023 version applied against a tree that
used pcim_iomap_regions() and had no SMBBAR_MMIO handling, so its context no
longer matched.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [ADLINK platform-specific workaround]
Signed-off-by: Adlink CCoE <dinesh.v@adlinktech.com>
Signed-off-by: Asuvath MS <asuvath.monikandan@adlinktech.com>

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-i801.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-i801.c
index 506d69b156f7..f87b9932213b 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-i801.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-i801.c
@@ -1576,8 +1576,14 @@ static int i801_probe(struct pci_dev *dev, const struct pci_device_id *id)
 		return -ENODEV;
 	}
 
-	if (i801_acpi_probe(priv))
-		return -ENODEV;
+	/*
+	 * ADLINK: the BIOS declares the SMBus I/O ports in its ACPI tables
+	 * and keeps accessing them at runtime. i801_acpi_probe() installs an
+	 * ACPI address-space handler that detects this, sets acpi_reserved and
+	 * makes every transaction return -EBUSY ("Driver SMBus register access
+	 * inhibited"), leaving the SEMA board management controller
+	 * unreachable. Skip the probe so the driver keeps ownership of the bus.
+	 */
 
 	if (pci_resource_flags(dev, SMBBAR_MMIO) & IORESOURCE_MEM)
 		bar = SMBBAR_MMIO;
